{"summary":"All four amendments should be accepted. The two plant-growth amendments identify the same real unit-mismatch defect from complementary angles, the test amendment closes the coverage gap that let it slip through, and the save migration amendment identifies a real stability-tracker reseeding bug on legacy load.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-4-4e504190","decision":"accept","rationale":"`plant_growth.rs` converts plant half-saturation parameters to mg/L, but `total_substrate_n()` and `total_substrate_p()` return raw substrate totals in mg and those totals are passed directly into `half_saturation()` at lines 60-61. That is a real dimensional mismatch. It drives the substrate factors close to 1.0 whenever the bed has any meaningful nutrient store, so substrate depletion barely affects limitation. Converting the substrate signal to a normalized density or keeping substrate on the legacy total-based constants are both technically valid fixes."},{"amendment_id":"fr-4-92b6a31f","decision":"accept","rationale":"The cited coverage gap is real. `plant_water_column_limitation_is_volume_invariant_at_fixed_concentration` explicitly clears substrate layers and sets `substrate_uptake_bias` to `0.0`, so it never exercises the substrate half-saturation path. Existing rooted-substrate tests check advantage/depletion under rich substrate, not that lower substrate availability reduces the limitation factor. A targeted test with nonzero substrate bias and varying substrate nutrient levels would catch this bug class."},{"amendment_id":"fr-4-0a3f3f58","decision":"accept","rationale":"The legacy migration path rescales water totals in `save.rs` but does not reseed `state.stability_tracker`. Because `stability_tracker` is `#[serde(default)]`, older saves that omit the field will deserialize to `StabilityTracker::default`, and the first `update_stability_tracker` call will compare migrated chemistry against those defaults instead of the loaded water state. Other constructors that rescale or initialize water explicitly call `seed_from_water`, so the migration path is an inconsistency and can produce a bogus instability spike."},{"amendment_id":"fr-4-6f70d3c3","decision":"accept","rationale":"This is the same underlying defect as `fr-4-4e504190`, but the invariance concern is also valid. The substrate factors use concentration-based half-saturation constants against absolute substrate totals, so root-feeding growth responds to total substrate mass rather than a normalized compartment quantity. That breaks the unit discipline already used in the water-column path and makes behavior depend incorrectly on geometry/footprint. The amendment identifies a real robustness problem and acceptable correction strategies."}]}